---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="speed-converter">
  <UnitConverter client:load dimension="speed" from="kmh" to="mph" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert a speed</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Type the speed into either field. The other field updates at once, so km/h to mph and mph to km/h use the same speed converter.</li>
      <li>Pick the units. The list has metric units, imperial units, nautical and aviation units, and the speed of light for physics questions.</li>
      <li>The chart below gives a full column of values, which is easier to scan than speed limits converted one at a time.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Speed conversion factors and mental shortcuts</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Conversion</th><th>Exact factor</th><th>Mental shortcut</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>km/h to mph</td><td>× 0.6213711922</td><td>Halve it, add 10 %</td></tr>
          <tr><td>mph to km/h</td><td>× 1.609344</td><td>Add 60 %</td></tr>
          <tr><td>m/s to km/h</td><td>× 3.6</td><td>Multiply by 3.6 exactly</td></tr>
          <tr><td>km/h to m/s</td><td>× 5/18</td><td>Divide by 3.6</td></tr>
          <tr><td>knots to km/h</td><td>× 1.852</td><td>Add 85 %</td></tr>
          <tr><td>knots to mph</td><td>× 1.150779448</td><td>Add 15 %</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The mile is exactly 1,609.344 m and the nautical mile is exactly 1,852 m, so each factor in the table
      is exact, and the converter uses the full value.
    </p>

    <h2>Speed limits at a glance</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>km/h</th><th>mph</th><th>Where you see it</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>30</td><td>18.6</td><td>Urban limits across Europe</td></tr>
          <tr><td>50</td><td>31.1</td><td>Standard built-up limit</td></tr>
          <tr><td>60</td><td>37.3</td><td>US residential and school zones (35–40 mph)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>80</td><td>49.7</td><td>Rural single carriageway</td></tr>
          <tr><td>100</td><td>62.1</td><td>Motorway in much of Europe</td></tr>
          <tr><td>112.7</td><td>70</td><td>UK motorway limit</td></tr>
          <tr><td>120</td><td>74.6</td><td>Spanish and French autoroute</td></tr>
          <tr><td>130</td><td>80.8</td><td>French motorway in dry weather</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Why Mach is not a fixed speed</h2>
    <p>
      Mach number is the ratio of an object's speed to the local speed of sound, and sound travels faster in
      warmer air. At sea level in the international standard atmosphere (15 °C), sound travels at
      340.29 m/s, the figure this converter uses. At 11 km, a typical airliner cruising altitude where the
      air is about −56 °C, it drops to about 295 m/s.
    </p>
    <p>
      The difference is large. An airliner cruising at Mach 0.85 is doing about 903 km/h at altitude, but
      the same Mach number at sea level is about 1,041 km/h. A Mach figure from this converter is a
      sea-level reference value, not an aviation calculation. Real flight planning uses the air
      temperature at altitude.
    </p>

    <h2>Knots, and why sailors and pilots use them</h2>
    <p>
      A knot is one nautical mile per hour, and a nautical mile is one minute of latitude. This makes
      navigation arithmetic on a chart easy: an aircraft that covers 300 nautical miles in an hour is doing
      300 knots and has crossed five degrees of latitude. The name comes from the knotted rope that sailors
      let out behind a ship to measure its speed against a sand timer.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
